#: default/searchbnf.conf.in:3371 
msgid ""
"LL, LLT, LLP, and LLP5 are univariate algorithms. LLB and BiLL are "
"bivariate algorithms.\\\n"
"LL is the simplest algorithm and computes the levels of the time series, "
"ie. each new state\\\n"
"equals the previous state plus a Gaussian noise.\\\n"
"LLT computes the levels plus the trend.\\\n"
"LLP takes into account the data's periodicity if it exists. You can set "
"the \\\n"
"period using the \"period\" option. You should set the period if you know"
" it \\\n"
"because it will likely be more accurate than letting the command estimate"
" the period.\\\n"
"If the you do not set the period, LLP will try to calculate it.\\\n"
"LLP5 combines LLT and LLP. If the time series is periodic, LLP5 computes "
"two \\\n"
"predictions, one using LLT and the other using LLP. Then LLP5 takes a "
"weighted \\\n"
"average of the two values and outputs that as its prediction. The "
"confidence \\\n"
"interval is also based on a weighted average of the variances of the \\\n"
"LLT and LLP algorithms.\\\n"
"LLB and BiLL are both bivariate local level algorithms. LLB predicts \\\n"
"one time series off the other. BiLL predicts both time series "
"simultaneously. \\\n"
"The key here is that the covariance of the two series is taken into "
"account."
msgstr ""

